  "content": {
    "id": "p15-q3-content",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Let $f(x)=ax^2-2x+c$. Find the rule of $f$ if $f(-1)=7$ and $f'(2)=6$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$f(x)=2x^2-2x+3$. The derivative condition gives $a=2$, then $f(-1)=7$ gives $c=3$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f'(x)&=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}\\\\f'(x)&=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{a(x+h)^2-2(x+h)+c-(ax^2-2x+c)}{h}\\\\f'(x)&=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{2axh+ah^2-2h}{h}\\\\f'(x)&=\\lim_{h\\to0}(2ax+ah-2)\\\\f'(x)&=2ax-2\\\\f'(2)&=4a-2=6\\\\a&=2\\\\f(-1)&=a+2+c=7\\\\c&=3\\\\f(x)&=2x^2-2x+3\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 86
  },
